From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:38:06 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ucode: decode the new timestamp messages on 32-bit

ucode patch 016 (for upstream), B74: on a 32-bit system with a 64-bit
time_t (the W-118's armv7 musl), ucode-mod-socket decoded the kernel's
SO_TIMESTAMP_NEW and SO_TIMESTAMPNS_NEW messages with the old layout,
because the _OLD fallbacks carry the _NEW values there and come first
in its table, so every receive timestamp came out in whole seconds
({ sec, usec: 0 }, { sec, nsec: 0 }). Look the _NEW entries up first,
and give st_timeval_new its own size. Still in upstream master.

On 32-bit this also undoes a step back from 0052 (2e0b25a2): before it
SO_TIMESTAMPNS arrived raw and a script could decode it itself; with it
the message was decoded, but wrongly, in whole seconds. 64-bit targets
(qualcommax: C-230, C-260, C-360, W-318) decode both right either way.

Bump PKG_RELEASE.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/package/utils/ucode/Makefile b/package/utils/ucode/Makefile
--- a/package/utils/ucode/Makefile
+++ b/package/utils/ucode/Makefile
@@ -8,7 +8,7 @@
 include $(TOPDIR)/rules.mk
 
 PKG_NAME:=ucode
-PKG_RELEASE:=2
+PKG_RELEASE:=3
 
 PKG_SOURCE_PROTO:=git
 PKG_SOURCE_URL=https://github.com/ucode-lang/ucode.git
diff --git a/package/utils/ucode/patches/016-socket-decode-the-new-timestamp-control-messages-on-time64.patch b/package/utils/ucode/patches/016-socket-decode-the-new-timestamp-control-messages-on-time64.patch
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/package/utils/ucode/patches/016-socket-decode-the-new-timestamp-control-messages-on-time64.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+From 6d66ffd3e303e1850c11b2ee77953a7324c3ce8a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
+Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:37:39 -0500
+Subject: [PATCH] socket: decode the new timestamp control messages on time64
+ systems
+
+On a 32-bit system with a 64-bit time_t, as musl has on 32-bit ARM, the
+libc's SO_TIMESTAMP and SO_TIMESTAMPNS are the kernel's _NEW values. The
+SO_TIMESTAMP_OLD and SO_TIMESTAMPNS_OLD fallbacks then carry those same
+values, and as the _OLD entries come first in the control message table,
+a _NEW message was decoded with the old layout: tv_sec from the low half
+of the 64-bit seconds and the fraction from the high half, which is 0.
+Every receive timestamp came out in whole seconds.
+
+Look the _NEW entries up first. Where the two values differ the order
+does not matter. Also give st_timeval_new its own size; it had the size
+of the old struct, which cut the decoding short of tv_usec.
+
+Seen on an Arista W-118 (IPQ4019, armv7, musl): SO_TIMESTAMP gave
+{ sec, usec: 0 } and SO_TIMESTAMPNS { sec, nsec: 0 } for every packet.
+
+Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
+Signed-off-by: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
+---
+ lib/socket.c | 8 +++++---
+ 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)
+
+diff --git a/lib/socket.c b/lib/socket.c
+index 16eae444..5ba91d0d 100644
+--- a/lib/socket.c
++++ b/lib/socket.c
+@@ -1680,7 +1680,7 @@ static struct_t st_timeval_old = {
+ # ifdef SO_TIMESTAMP_NEW
+ struct timeval_new_local { int64_t tv_sec; int64_t tv_usec; };
+ static struct_t st_timeval_new = {
+-	.size = sizeof(struct timeval_old_local),
++	.size = sizeof(struct timeval_new_local),
+ 	.members = (member_t []){
+ 		STRUCT_MEMBER(timeval_new_local, tv, sec, DT_SIGNED),
+ 		STRUCT_MEMBER(timeval_new_local, tv, usec, DT_SIGNED),
+@@ -1906,14 +1906,16 @@ static cmsgtype_t cmsgtypes[] = {
+ #if defined(__linux__)
+ 	{ SOL_PACKET, PACKET_AUXDATA, &st_tpacket_auxdata },
+ 
+-	{ SOL_SOCKET, SO_TIMESTAMP_OLD, &st_timeval_old },
++	/* the _NEW entries first: with a 64-bit time_t on a 32-bit system the
++	 * _OLD fallbacks above carry the _NEW values */
+ # ifdef SO_TIMESTAMP_NEW
+ 	{ SOL_SOCKET, SO_TIMESTAMP_NEW, &st_timeval_new },
+ # endif
+-	{ SOL_SOCKET, SO_TIMESTAMPNS_OLD, &st_timespec_old },
+ # ifdef SO_TIMESTAMPNS_NEW
+ 	{ SOL_SOCKET, SO_TIMESTAMPNS_NEW, &st_timespec_new },
+ # endif
++	{ SOL_SOCKET, SO_TIMESTAMP_OLD, &st_timeval_old },
++	{ SOL_SOCKET, SO_TIMESTAMPNS_OLD, &st_timespec_old },
+ 
+ 	{ SOL_SOCKET, SCM_CREDENTIALS, &st_ucred },
+ 	{ SOL_SOCKET, SCM_RIGHTS, CV_FDS },
+-- 
+2.43.0
+
